```java
protected void burstToken(String token, boolean stopAtNonOption)
{
    for (int i = 1; i < token.length(); i++)
    {
        String ch = String.valueOf(token.charAt(i));

        if (options.hasOption(ch))
        {
            tokens.add("-" + ch);
            currentOption = options.getOption(ch);

            if (currentOption.hasArg() && (token.length() != (i + 1)))
            {
                tokens.add(token.substring(i + 1));

                break;
            }
        }
        else if (stopAtNonOption)
        {
            process(token.substring(i));
            break;
        }
        else
        {
            tokens.add(token);
            break;
        }
    }
}
```

In this method, the token is iterated over character by character, and if an option exists for a particular character, it is added to the list of tokens. If the current option has an argument and there are characters remaining in the token, the remaining characters are added as a separate token.

The issue observed in the failing test and the bug report suggests that the behavior in `burstToken` may be contributing to the problem of splitting tokens incorrectly after encountering an unrecognized long option. Further investigation into how tokens are processed and split within the loop of `burstToken` may provide insights into the root cause of the issue.

Taking into account the handling of unrecognized tokens and the token splitting behavior, a fix may involve adjusting the logic within the `burstToken` method to ensure that tokens are processed and added correctly without unintended splitting.

